# Pixi-ROS Workspace

This ROS {distro} workspace is configured to use [Pixi](https://pixi.sh) for dependency management.

## Getting Started

### 1. Install dependencies

Run this from the workspace:

```bash
pixi-ros install
```

`pixi-ros` refreshes dependencies from your `package.xml` files and runs `pixi install`.
It reuses your root `pixi.toml` and preserves existing configuration.
It can also create the manifest for a new workspace, so you don't need to run `init` first.
Pixi must already be installed.

The distro is inferred from existing ROS dependencies or channels.
If it can't be inferred, you'll be prompted; `ROS_DISTRO` can supply the prompt default.
Resolve conflicting distro settings before installing.
A new workspace defaults to the current platform, while existing workspace platforms are retained.
Use `--distro {distro}` to select the distro explicitly, repeated `--platform` options for target platforms, and repeated `--channel` options for additional channels.

If you've already reviewed the manifest and only need to install its dependencies, use `pixi install` directly.
Commit `pixi.toml` and `pixi.lock` so others can reproduce the environment.

### 2. Activate the environment

```bash
pixi shell
```

This starts a shell with the ROS environment activated.
You can also use `pixi run <command>` without entering the shell.

### 3. Build and test the workspace

Installing dependencies doesn't build your local ROS packages.
Run the build separately:

```bash
pixi run build
pixi run test
```

After building, run `pixi-ros install` again to register the workspace setup script for subsequent environment activations.

### Optional: Generate the manifest without installing

```bash
pixi-ros init --distro {distro}
```

`init` configures the manifest and generates this guide, but doesn't install dependencies.
The advanced `init --dev` mode writes workspace source dependencies into `[dev]` for Pixi to resolve at install time.
`pixi-ros install` preserves existing source dependencies and doesn't generate a README.

## Adding dependencies

When you change dependencies in your `package.xml` files, refresh and install them:

```bash
pixi-ros install
```

To add a conda package directly:

```bash
pixi add <package-name>
```

To add a PyPI package:

```bash
pixi add --pypi <package-name>
```

## Unavailable packages

If `pixi.toml` contains commented-out lines marked `# NOT FOUND`, those packages couldn't be resolved.
`pixi-ros install` won't report a complete installation while dependencies are unresolved or unavailable.
Fix the reported dependencies and run it again.

1. **Use a custom channel** if the package lives elsewhere:
   ```bash
   pixi-ros install --distro {distro} --channel https://prefix.dev/my-channel
   ```
2. **Check the package name**: verify spelling in your `package.xml`.
3. **Add a mapping** in `pixi-ros/*.yaml` if the conda package uses a different name.
4. **Add package source to the workspace** if you need to build it locally.
5. **Contribute to RoboStack** if the package is missing from the default ROS channel:
   - [ros-humble](https://github.com/RoboStack/ros-humble)
   - [ros-jazzy](https://github.com/RoboStack/ros-jazzy)
   - [ros-kilted](https://github.com/RoboStack/ros-kilted)
   - [ros-lyrical](https://github.com/RoboStack/ros-lyrical)
   - [ros-rolling](https://github.com/RoboStack/ros-rolling)

## Common issues

### Build fails

1. Make sure all dependencies are installed: `pixi install`
2. Clean and rebuild: `pixi run clean && pixi run build`

### `ros2` commands not found

Run commands through pixi: `pixi run <command>` or enter the shell with `pixi shell`.

### Active ROS environment conflict

If you have a ROS installation sourced in your shell, it can conflict with Pixi's environment.
Remove or comment out lines like these from `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc`, then restart your shell:

```bash
# source /opt/ros/{distro}/setup.bash
# source ~/ros_ws/install/setup.bash
```

Pixi manages ROS activation, so you don't need to source another ROS installation manually.

